WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.510
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:09.660
This work is about OpenAI’s new technique
that teaches a robot arm to dexterously manipulate

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.050
a block to a target state.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:17.939
And in this project, they did one of my favorite
things, which is, first, training an AI within

00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:22.460
a simulation, and then, deploying it into
the real world.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.259
And in the best case scenario, this knowledge
from the simulation will actually generalize

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:28.650
to the real world.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:33.700
However, while we are in the simulation, we
can break free from the limitations of worldly

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:39.220
things, such as hardware, movement speed,
or, even time itself.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.860
So how is that possible?

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.620
The limitation on the number of experiments
we can run in a simulation is bounded by not

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:51.600
our time, which is scarce, but how powerful
our hardware is, which is abundant as it is

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.620
accelerating at nearly exponential pace.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.530
And, this is the reason why OpenAI’s and
DeepMind’s AI was able to train for 200

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.659
years worth of games before first playing
a human pro player.

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:09.979
This sounds great, but a simulation is always
more crude than the real world, so do we know

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:15.340
for sure that we created something that will
indeed be useful in the real world, and not

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.700
just in the simulation?

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.810
Let’s try an analogy.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.649
Think of the machine as a student, and the
simulation would be its textbook that it learns

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:24.649
from.

00:01:24.649 --> 00:01:29.450
If the textbook contains only a few trivial
problems to learn from, when the day of the

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:34.180
exam comes, if the exam is any good, the student
will fail.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:39.159
The exam is the equivalent of deploying the
machine into the real world, and apparently,

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:41.770
the real world is a damn good exam.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:45.520
So how can we prepare a student to do well
on this exam?

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.240
Well, we have to provide them with a textbook
that contains not only a lot of problems,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.560
but also a diverse set of challenges as well.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.780
This is what machine learning researchers
call domain randomization.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.590
This means that we teach the AI program in
different virtual worlds, and in each one

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:09.470
of them, we change parameters like how fast
the hand is, what color and weight the cube

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.080
is, and more.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:16.400
This is a proper textbook, which means that
after this kind of training, this AI can deal

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.390
with new and unexpected situations.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:24.420
The knowledge that it has obtained is so general
that we can change even the geometry of the

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.480
target object and the machine will still be
able to manipulate it correctly.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.890
Outstanding.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.670
To implement this idea, scientists at OpenAI
trained not one agent, but a selection of

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.280
agents in these randomized environments.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.310
The first main component of this system is
a pose estimator.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:48.360
This module looks at the cube from three angles
and predicts the position and orientation

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.900
of the block, and is implemented through a
convolutional neural network.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.480
The advantage of this is that as we can generate
a near-infinite amount of training data ourselves.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.960
You can see here that when the AI looks at
real images, it is only a few degrees worse

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.670
than in the simulation when estimating angles,
which is the case of the excellent textbook.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.850
I would not be surprised if this accuracy
exceeds the capabilities of an ordinary human,

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.540
given that it can perform this many times
within a second.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.870
Then, the next part is choosing what the next
action should be.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:28.190
Of course, we seek to rotate this cube in
a way that brings us closer to our objective.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.740
This is done by a reinforcement learning technique,
which uses similar modules as OpenAI’s previous

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.690
algorithm that learned to play DOTA2 really
well.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:41.410
Another testament to how general these learning
algorithms are.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.710
I also recommend checking out OpenAI’s video
on this work in the video description.

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:51.620
Now, I always read in the comments here on
Youtube that many of you are longing for more.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:57.610
